    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Description\n\nI've been using the following textobject with mini.ai to select the whole buffer:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\ne = function() -- Whole buffer\r\n\tlocal from = { line = 1, col = 1 }\r\n\tlocal last_line_length = #vim.fn.getline(\"$\")\r\n\tlocal to = {\r\n\t\tline = vim.fn.line(\"$\"),\r\n\t\tcol = last_line_length == 0 and 1 or last_line_length,\r\n\t}\r\n\treturn {\r\n\t\tfrom = from,\r\n\t\tto = to,\r\n\t}\r\nend,\r\n```\r\n\r\nAdditionally, I use [vim-sort-motion](https://github.com/christoomey/vim-sort-motion) to map `gs` to sort. vim-sort-motion uses `:sort` to sort a textobject when it is linewise (when `line` is passed to the operatorfunc) but tries to split the region by a delimiter and sort using `sort()` when the region is charwise (so, it could sort `5,2,1` into `1,2,5`).\r\n\r\nI get an error when I run `gsae` to sort buffer contents because, as far as I can tell, textobjects defined by mini.ai are always defined as charwise regions.\r\n\r\nIn [vim-textobj-user](https://github.com/kana/vim-textobj-user), you could define the visual command when creating a region (passing `V` to define a linewise region or `v` to define a charwise region). I know I can pass `vis_mode` to `MiniAi.select_textobject()`, but is there (or could there be) a way to define the preferred `vis_mode` for a textobject, so that some could always be linewise?",
    "closed_at": "2023-06-29T13:11:10Z",
